	...
> The biggest disadvantage would be low HP, unless they are allowed
> to drink potions/dance with nurses.

	You're thinking of these as basically humans with minor
changes.  Instead, think of these as large clockwork devices,
decide what people will expect (or find reasonable), then think
of how to implement this in such a way that automatons are play-
able.

	For example, I don't expect automatons to heal normally, and 
I don't expect them to grow tougher.  But, they could certainly start
with arbitrary toughness, right?

	Option 1:  you start at 500 HP.  You only heal after you 
get a repair kit or tool kit.  These do not appear randomly.  The 
quest nemesis drops one and/or you can wish for one.  (But note 
that demons ignore automatons, so you can't get an early wish 
by dipping.)  

	Option 2:  you start at 500 HP.  You never heal.  But, 
you can build pet automatons for [shrug] 500 wt of iron, 20 GP,
a silver item of any size, and maybe one other ingredient.  Your
first pet would be called "JH 1".  When your first automaton is
destroyed, "automaton JH 1 takes over the lead", and is renamed
"JH".

	Set up the "recipe" so that having a backup by minetown is
very rare, by the quest is a bit unusual, by the Castle is fairly
common, and you'll make 2-10 over the course of an ascension.

	Option 3:  just give 'em 20 HPs per level.  IMO, this is 
dumb, just because I don't expect automatons to get tougher.  Still,
the same objection applies to people....

	I like option 2 more than option 1.  The disadvantage to 
both options is that they require playtesting and tweaking.  I can
shovel out numbers like "500 HP", but that isn't the same as 500
HP being playable.  


